	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: Allograft rejection was defined as “moderate or severe acute cellular rejection (ACR).” On the held-out test set, the AI-ECG achieved “AUC of 0.84 (95% CI: 0.78–0.90),” sensitivity 95%, specificity 52.6%, PPV 5.1%, and NPV 99.7% using threshold 0.4. The prospective proof-of-concept study showed AUC 0.78 and sensitivity 100%, but the prompt asks for held-out test/validation-set metrics, so the primary extracted model performance is from the retrospective test set. Figure 3 shows the ROC curve, and Figure 4 shows subgroup AUC performance; performance was better within 7 days of biopsy (0.87) than >7 days (0.77). Calibration and DCA were not reported.  ￼  ￼

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a retrospective study from “three Mayo Clinic US sites,” and patients were restricted to those with digital ECGs and EMBs within 30 days, which may limit representativeness and introduce selection bias.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were standardized 12-lead ECG waveforms acquired at 500 Hz and recorded before EMB, so the predictor measurement process was objective and clinically plausible.  ￼  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: ACR was defined using ISHLT criteria, and the final classification groups were “reviewed and approved by a cardiovascular pathologist and transplant cardiologist.”  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include patient-level partitioning, held-out testing, and hyperparameter search, but uncertainty remains because missing-data handling for clinical variables was not described, clustering was simplified by assuming repeated EMB independence, and no calibration analysis was reported.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The article directly addresses AI-based detection of heart transplant rejection using a non-invasive model in transplant recipients, which is closely aligned with the review question.  ￼

